WEBVTT

00:30.000 --> 00:45.280
Near the center of modern-day Fort Bliss, Texas, stands a small frontier army post,

00:45.280 --> 00:49.800
a recreation of the fort as it was over 100 years ago.

00:49.800 --> 00:58.860
During those years, Fort Bliss has been the home of the infantrymen, the horse cavalrymen,

00:58.860 --> 01:07.480
the anti-aircraft artillerymen, and today, the air defense missilemen.

01:07.480 --> 01:13.120
The simple army outpost of 100 years ago and the imposing facilities of modern Fort Bliss

01:13.120 --> 01:15.400
would seem to have little in common.

01:15.400 --> 01:20.080
Yet through the decades, the Army's basic mission in the Fort Bliss area has remained

01:20.080 --> 01:35.280
the same, defense of the United States.

01:35.280 --> 01:40.720
The Army's role in this southwest area began during the Mexican War when Colonel Alexander

01:40.720 --> 01:45.880
Donovan led troops into the Paso del Norte, or Pass of the North.

01:45.880 --> 01:50.880
Not long afterwards, with expanding national boundaries, soldiers surveyed the first wagon

01:50.880 --> 01:57.680
road from San Antonio to what is now El Paso.

01:57.680 --> 02:00.440
Then came the wagon trains pushing westward.

02:00.440 --> 02:07.560
They had to be protected, as did lonely frontier settlements.

02:07.560 --> 02:12.960
Protection was also needed by those solitary wanderers of the west, the trappers and prospectors,

02:12.960 --> 02:18.080
as well as the isolated cattlemen.

02:18.080 --> 02:22.560
The lone stagecoach constantly faced Indian attack.

02:22.560 --> 02:31.720
Yes, for the Army, the years on the frontier before the Civil War were active ones indeed.

02:31.720 --> 02:38.920
When the Civil War began, Fort Bliss was occupied by the Confederacy.

02:38.920 --> 02:44.080
Here in the 1870s, no troops were assigned to the border area, and the lawless element

02:44.080 --> 02:48.120
terrorized the settlers.

02:48.120 --> 02:55.840
In 1878, the troops returned to restore order.

02:55.840 --> 03:04.680
In 1914, Brigadier General John J. Pershing assumed command of Fort Bliss.

03:04.680 --> 03:10.920
Pancho Villa, the Mexican Revolutionary, posed a threat to border communities.

03:10.920 --> 03:16.440
To maintain peace, more troops were needed, so state militia was called to duty, and by

03:16.440 --> 03:23.840
1916, some 60,000 troops were at Fort Bliss.

03:23.840 --> 03:28.440
Later that year, with permission of the Mexican government, Pershing led the punitive expedition

03:28.440 --> 03:33.120
into Mexico.

03:33.120 --> 03:38.880
This expedition marked the first occasion that American troops used aircraft, a development

03:38.880 --> 03:43.720
that would mean much to Fort Bliss in the future.

03:43.720 --> 03:48.540
Came World War I, and the fort was garrisoned by the 15th Cavalry.

03:48.540 --> 03:51.760
Men were trained here for the front lines in France.

03:51.760 --> 03:59.100
Many outstanding leaders in World War I saw duty at Fort Bliss.

03:59.100 --> 04:05.840
From the period between the two great wars, the famed first cavalry was stationed here.

04:05.840 --> 04:10.880
In 1940, on the eve of World War II, a new branch of the service was brought to Fort

04:10.880 --> 04:16.040
Bliss, the anti-aircraft artillery.

04:16.040 --> 04:23.000
Gun crews trained here served throughout the world.

04:23.000 --> 04:28.880
In 1945, when captured V-2 missiles were brought to this country, a brand new kind of unit

04:28.880 --> 04:31.080
was activated at Fort Bliss.

04:31.080 --> 04:36.060
The first anti-aircraft and guided missile battalion, organized specifically for missile

04:36.060 --> 04:38.000
research and development.

04:38.000 --> 04:44.240
As early as 1944, the first American air defense missile had been conceived by Fort Bliss anti-aircraft

04:44.240 --> 04:49.920
artillery officers.

04:49.920 --> 04:56.680
Nine years later, in 1953, an extensive development effort paid off when the Nike Ajax air defense

04:56.680 --> 04:59.480
missile was placed in the hands of the troops.

04:59.480 --> 05:03.360
The Army air defense artillery had entered the missile age.

05:03.360 --> 05:05.360
But why missiles?

05:05.360 --> 05:06.760
Here's why.

05:06.760 --> 05:12.040
With the speed of modern aircraft, conventional artillery, even using radar and computers,

05:12.040 --> 05:17.300
must be aimed at intercept points over five miles ahead of the oncoming target, too long

05:17.300 --> 05:20.160
a distance to be practical.

05:20.160 --> 05:26.960
Besides, evasive action by the target would make a hit next to impossible, since the projectile

05:26.960 --> 05:30.520
cannot maneuver.

05:30.520 --> 05:34.760
Guided missiles were the only answer.

05:34.760 --> 05:39.760
In a few years, Fort Bliss assumed its modern role as home of the U.S. Army Air Defense

05:39.760 --> 05:40.760
Center.

05:40.760 --> 05:45.760
Here, the air defense school teaches today's missile men all the complex skills needed

05:45.760 --> 05:51.800
to defend either the 50 states or our field armies against air attack.

05:51.800 --> 06:00.440
Advanced individual training for troops assigned to the air defense artillery is also provided.

06:00.440 --> 06:05.400
Among auxiliary and tenant organizations are the U.S. Army Combat Developments Command

06:05.400 --> 06:12.760
Air Defense Agency, the U.S. Army Air Defense Board, the Army Air Defense Human Research

06:12.760 --> 06:20.320
Unit, the Combat Development Command's Institute of Nuclear Studies, the Defense Language Institute,

06:20.320 --> 06:27.920
the Allied Student Battalion, the Federal Republic of Germany's Air Defense School,

06:27.920 --> 06:33.520
and several artillery groups who support training in the operation of air defense weapons.

06:33.520 --> 06:39.760
Now, let's take a look at how the Air Defense Center fits into the big picture.

06:39.760 --> 06:44.880
First, protection of the North American continent against air attack is the mission of the North

06:44.880 --> 06:51.960
American Air Defense Command, a combined Canadian-American command controlling land, sea, and air forces

06:51.960 --> 06:58.000
from a combat operations center under Cheyenne Mountain near Colorado Springs, Colorado.

06:58.000 --> 07:03.800
The Army's role in this joint operation is carried out by the U.S. Army Air Defense Command.

07:03.800 --> 07:09.160
Specifically, the Army's job is to defend the skies over more than 100 key population

07:09.160 --> 07:15.680
and industrial centers of the United States.

07:15.680 --> 07:22.080
To do this, the Mobile Hawk, primarily a field weapon for defense against low-flying aircraft,

07:22.080 --> 07:27.280
can be made available to protect any location in the United States proper when necessary.

07:27.280 --> 07:33.200
Permanently on guard stands the reliable Nike Hercules, ready to defend against high-altitude

07:33.200 --> 07:36.560
threats.

07:36.560 --> 07:41.800
This is how a Nike site would operate should an enemy threaten our country.

07:41.800 --> 07:46.860
Upon detection by the North American Air Defense Command's warning system, the enemy's number

07:46.860 --> 07:51.840
and location, direction of flight, and speed of travel are flashed to headquarters.

07:51.840 --> 07:58.140
Instantly, this information is passed on through division direction centers to Army, Navy,

07:58.140 --> 08:04.920
and Air Force Air Defense Commands.

08:04.920 --> 08:09.720
Assume that this Nike Hercules site is in the flight path of the enemy aircraft.

08:09.720 --> 08:14.640
Alerted to the enemy's approach, the Nike Hercules search radar detects the intruders

08:14.640 --> 08:18.840
and determines their location.

08:18.840 --> 08:23.120
Target tracking radar is assigned to track the enemy and feed position information into

08:23.120 --> 08:27.320
a computer.

08:27.320 --> 08:31.480
The particular missile selected to fire is guided toward the target by a missile tracking

08:31.480 --> 08:38.960
radar, which sends steering orders to the supersonic missile.

08:38.960 --> 08:43.560
The computer produces the steering orders by continuously comparing target and missile

08:43.560 --> 08:51.360
positions.

08:51.360 --> 08:55.560
Search radar continues to scan the sky for enemy targets.

08:55.560 --> 08:58.480
Additional missiles are ready to fire immediately.

08:58.480 --> 09:02.840
To meet the second big challenge to Army Air Defense forces, protection of the field

09:02.840 --> 09:08.000
army, Army Air Defense weapons will be deployed as represented on this painting.

09:08.000 --> 09:12.640
Of course, distances will be greater.

09:12.640 --> 09:18.860
Automatic artillery weapons will be placed well to the front of the battle area.

09:18.860 --> 09:23.600
They will defend air corridors where radar cannot reach due to such terrain features

09:23.600 --> 09:26.880
as narrow valleys.

09:26.880 --> 09:31.720
Farther back, Hawk units will wait for unfriendly aircraft.

09:31.720 --> 09:36.640
Still farther back, the Nike Hercules will be ready to attack any intruder getting past

09:36.640 --> 09:40.720
the forward defenses.

09:40.720 --> 09:46.040
Though our largely automatic air defense system is truly a technical marvel, highly trained

09:46.040 --> 09:49.840
men are vital to its maintenance and operation.

09:49.840 --> 09:54.720
Training these men is the job of the center's U.S. Army Air Defense School.

09:54.720 --> 09:59.140
The students, most of whom are volunteers, are carefully selected by means of aptitude

09:59.140 --> 10:01.040
and intelligence tests.

10:01.040 --> 10:06.400
The jobs these men are being trained for require a high degree of technical competence.

10:06.400 --> 10:11.400
The school conducts over 60 courses at once, ranging from a few days to more than a full

10:11.400 --> 10:12.400
year.

10:12.400 --> 10:18.360
The specialties taught are many.

10:18.360 --> 10:23.640
For instance, electronics, as applied to complex radar, missile guidance, and computer systems,

10:23.640 --> 10:28.960
are a major field of study.

10:28.960 --> 10:34.160
Military tactics and leadership are also of paramount interest.

10:34.160 --> 10:39.760
Graduates of the school join the ranks as line and staff air defense officers, electronic

10:39.760 --> 10:51.040
warfare specialists, Hawk and Nike Hercules missile technicians, automatic weapons specialists,

10:51.040 --> 11:01.560
fire distribution system operators, and maintenance technicians.

11:01.560 --> 11:07.000
It is obvious that the control and coordination of widely separated air defense weapons demand

11:07.000 --> 11:11.360
reliable communication systems.

11:11.360 --> 11:15.960
To teach maintenance and operation of such systems is a major mission of the air defense

11:15.960 --> 11:17.360
school.

11:17.360 --> 11:25.320
Here, students watch a diorama demonstrating operations of the ANG-5, or BIRD-E, fire distribution

11:25.320 --> 11:26.320
system.

11:26.320 --> 11:33.920
BIRD-E, an acronym for Battery Integration and Radar Display Equipment, coordinates operations

11:33.920 --> 11:39.640
of missile fire units by conducting a continuous high-speed exchange of tactical information

11:39.640 --> 11:45.600
among all fire units and higher air defense commands.

11:45.600 --> 11:51.040
This information includes position and identity of all aircraft within the defense area, the

11:51.040 --> 11:56.800
status of each fire unit, and commands, such as assignment of specific targets to specific

11:56.800 --> 11:59.280
fire units.

11:59.280 --> 12:04.160
To see how BIRD-E maintains effective control of a tactical situation, let's assume that

12:04.160 --> 12:08.840
an intruding aircraft has been detected by the radar net of the North American Air Defense

12:08.840 --> 12:14.360
Command, NORAD.

12:14.360 --> 12:18.280
Information on the hostile aircraft's position and identity is flashed to BIRD-E.

12:18.280 --> 12:22.560
This information, along with that supplied by local surveillance radar, is displayed

12:22.560 --> 12:25.360
on BIRD-E consoles in the form of symbols.

12:25.360 --> 12:30.320
The console operator monitors the progress of the enemy aircraft and corrects or updates

12:30.320 --> 12:32.400
the data as necessary.

12:32.400 --> 12:37.440
All target information displayed on the BIRD-E console is also displayed at each fire unit.

12:37.440 --> 12:42.680
Thus, each fire unit commander has an accurate picture of the overall air situation.

12:42.680 --> 12:47.080
The BIRD-E console operator decides which fire unit is best situated to engage a hostile

12:47.080 --> 12:52.160
aircraft and assigns the target to that fire unit.

12:52.160 --> 13:01.840
At this time, the battery commander of the fire unit starts tracking the target by radar

13:01.840 --> 13:16.680
in preparation for firing a missile.

13:16.680 --> 13:21.440
Now that the enemy aircraft has been destroyed, the fire unit transmits a message to BIRD-E

13:21.440 --> 13:24.800
indicating that it is ready to engage additional targets.

13:24.800 --> 13:30.200
Also, the kill information is relayed by BIRD-E to higher command and the tracking information

13:30.200 --> 13:37.000
on the destroyed aircraft is cleared from the system.

13:37.000 --> 13:42.000
Now let's visit a class engaged in study of actual hardware.

13:42.000 --> 13:49.960
Gentlemen, this is the general purpose console for the missile mentor fire distribution system.

13:49.960 --> 13:54.400
This console automatically exchanges data with all other elements within the area to

13:54.400 --> 13:57.120
be defended.

13:57.120 --> 14:02.080
Immediately displayed gives the Army Air Defense commander an instant picture of the air battle.

14:02.080 --> 14:06.700
As the battle takes place by watching the console, he can immediately determine such

14:06.700 --> 14:11.760
things as the number of enemy aircraft present or when a kill has been made.

14:11.760 --> 14:16.760
Which firing unit should fire its missiles and the time of firing.

14:16.760 --> 14:20.960
So began a class of future fire distribution system maintenance men.

14:20.960 --> 14:26.080
But a fire distribution system includes many items besides the consoles.

14:26.080 --> 14:34.760
For instance, classes are held on radar data processing equipment, memory storing units,

14:34.760 --> 14:44.120
several types of surveillance radar, computing equipment, and automatic data transmission

14:44.120 --> 14:48.280
systems.

14:48.280 --> 14:55.200
Another major part of the air defense school is the low altitude air defense department.

14:55.200 --> 14:59.880
Here a new class get their first lesson in maintenance of the twin 40 millimeter self

14:59.880 --> 15:01.640
propelled gun.

15:01.640 --> 15:07.600
This lightly armored automatic artillery weapon was designed for air defense at the division

15:07.600 --> 15:08.880
level.

15:08.880 --> 15:14.760
But because of its rapid rate of fire, it has proved itself a valuable support weapon

15:14.760 --> 15:16.960
against ground targets.

15:16.960 --> 15:22.040
You men are about to receive training in the operation of a weapon made famous as the quad

15:22.040 --> 15:24.560
50 in World War II.

15:24.560 --> 15:30.000
As you can see, four caliber 50 machine guns are supported by a power driven mount.

15:30.000 --> 15:35.920
Now everybody take your stations and we will run through a dry run to familiarize you with

15:35.920 --> 15:37.360
this weapon.

15:37.360 --> 15:39.020
This is the red eye.

15:39.020 --> 15:44.480
It is a man transportable shoulder fired air defense missile.

15:44.480 --> 15:49.940
It will be used to protect frontline troops against such targets as jets, helicopters,

15:49.940 --> 15:52.400
and reconnaissance aircraft.

15:52.400 --> 15:57.320
This is the Vulcan, an automatic 20 millimeter gun with an extremely high rate of fire.

15:57.320 --> 16:01.120
It can be used against both aerial and ground targets.

16:01.120 --> 16:06.400
This linkless feed system stores 1200 rounds in ready to fire condition.

16:06.400 --> 16:10.160
Against aircraft, 3000 rounds are fired per minute.

16:10.160 --> 16:14.800
Against ground targets, the Vulcan is set for 1000 rounds per minute.

16:14.800 --> 16:18.840
When deployed in the field, the Vulcan will be used in conjunction with the Chaparral

16:18.840 --> 16:19.840
system.

16:19.840 --> 16:26.320
The Chaparral system uses an infrared guidance system to defend the forward battle area against

16:26.320 --> 16:28.240
low flying aircraft.

16:28.240 --> 16:34.560
The system is fully mobile, has a top speed of 40 miles per hour, and can cruise for about

16:34.560 --> 16:37.260
300 miles.

16:37.260 --> 16:41.680
The air defense school devotes a large part of its effort to training officers and men

16:41.680 --> 16:45.200
in the operation and maintenance of the Hawk missile system.

16:45.200 --> 16:49.760
The Hawk, designed for defense against sneak attack by low flying aircraft, works like

16:49.760 --> 16:52.480
this.

16:52.480 --> 16:58.200
Two acquisition radars, one for higher altitude and one for extreme low level search, detect

16:58.200 --> 17:04.200
the intruder, a jet drawn aircraft.

17:04.200 --> 17:08.640
In the battery controlled central van, the tactical control officer watches the radar

17:08.640 --> 17:13.460
display of target speed and direction.

17:13.460 --> 17:19.400
At the proper time, he assigns the target to a Hawk fire unit.

17:19.400 --> 17:23.840
The illuminator radar, facing in the direction from which the threat is approaching, searches

17:23.840 --> 17:29.840
a small area of sky.

17:29.840 --> 17:37.360
Now its beam finds the target, locks on, and automatically tracks.

17:37.360 --> 17:52.120
Next, a specific launcher is selected, slaved to the radar, and the firing button pushed.

17:52.120 --> 17:57.000
The missile is guided by signals directly from the illuminator radar and homes on signals

17:57.000 --> 18:11.520
reflected from the target.

18:11.520 --> 18:16.920
The Hawk missile has proved so reliable and accurate that it has been adopted as a primary

18:16.920 --> 18:21.960
air defense weapon by the Marine Corps and several NATO nations, whose maintenance and

18:21.960 --> 18:31.480
operational personnel are trained at Fort Bliss.

18:31.480 --> 18:36.000
But successful as the Hawk has proved to be, improvements are being made to enable the

18:36.000 --> 18:43.400
system to meet battlefield requirements with even more effectiveness.

18:43.400 --> 18:50.080
Another major part of the air defense school is the high altitude missile department.

18:50.080 --> 18:56.000
Here an officer's class is instructed on operation of the Nike Hercules guided missile system.

18:56.000 --> 19:01.480
Nike Hercules is a long range weapon capable of destroying an entire formation of hostile

19:01.480 --> 19:02.840
aircraft.

19:02.840 --> 19:08.360
It is reliable and extremely accurate, having hit targets traveling at supersonic speeds,

19:08.360 --> 19:15.240
at distances greater than 75 miles, and at altitudes up to 150,000 feet.

19:15.240 --> 19:20.560
As you can now see, we have our target presented on our scopes.

19:20.560 --> 19:27.720
Now it is the job of our operators to go and place this target into a track condition.

19:27.720 --> 19:32.160
Some of these officer students may attend the school's guided missile systems officer

19:32.160 --> 19:38.600
course to study science and engineering fields relating to missalry and space technology.

19:38.600 --> 19:44.480
The purpose is to train staff officers capable of analyzing past, present, and future scientific

19:44.480 --> 19:49.080
developments as they relate to concepts of modern warfare.

19:49.080 --> 19:53.400
Officers with such training are essential to effective development and use of highly

19:53.400 --> 19:55.200
complex missile systems.

19:55.200 --> 20:02.200
This range has some elevation information to our computer.

20:02.200 --> 20:06.840
The Nike Hercules was originally designed for installation at permanent sites, but to

20:06.840 --> 20:11.320
provide a high altitude defense for our army in the field, the system was made mobile.

20:11.320 --> 20:16.720
For a brief look to the future, the Spartan missile is being developed to defend our strategic

20:16.720 --> 20:25.520
retaliatory forces, such as intercontinental ballistic missile sites, against enemy warheads.

20:25.520 --> 20:29.120
This is Sprint, Spartan's smaller partner.

20:29.120 --> 20:32.960
Sprint and Spartan are the two missiles of the Army's safeguard ballistic missile defense

20:32.960 --> 20:39.680
system.

20:39.680 --> 20:44.800
To assure that enough highly trained technicians will be ready to man the complex system, the

20:44.800 --> 20:51.040
safeguard central training facility has been set up at the air defense school.

20:51.040 --> 20:56.120
Another air defense system currently under development is the SAM-D, designed for both

20:56.120 --> 21:01.920
battlefield and continental deployment against low, medium, and high altitude aircraft and

21:01.920 --> 21:04.320
short range missiles.

21:04.320 --> 21:09.640
The SAM-D missile is expected to replace eventually the Nike Hercules and a portion of our military

21:09.640 --> 21:16.840
Hawke systems, both at home and overseas.

21:16.840 --> 21:21.280
To be fully trained, the military missile man must actually fire his weapon.

21:21.280 --> 21:26.840
For this purpose, the air defense center operates McGregor Range, the largest air defense missile

21:26.840 --> 21:31.160
training range in the free world.

21:31.160 --> 21:35.980
Here the Army training center gives advanced individual training in firing the various

21:35.980 --> 21:40.680
air defense weapons against a variety of targets.

21:40.680 --> 21:57.120
For instance, the twin 40s are put through their paces.

21:57.120 --> 22:21.980
Here the quad 50s go into action.

22:21.980 --> 22:33.680
As an instructor looks on, red-eye, one man defense system goes against an aerial attack.

22:33.680 --> 22:39.200
The Army's old reliable Gatling gun has been reincarnated, so to speak, by a modern weapon,

22:39.200 --> 22:40.200
the Vulcan.

22:40.200 --> 23:09.860
Chaparral, companion weapon to the Vulcan, seeks a flying target.

23:09.860 --> 23:19.200
The fast-moving drone is greatly slowed by the camera.

23:19.200 --> 23:27.940
Since 1960, the range has supported annual practice as well as advanced training firings

23:27.940 --> 23:32.780
of air defense missiles by U.S. and NATO units from throughout the free world.

23:32.780 --> 23:38.780
The Hawke search radars look for the target, but there's no target in the sky.

23:38.780 --> 23:48.700
Instead, the equipment in this radar signal simulator station supplies the target electronically.

23:48.700 --> 23:54.020
The signals generated here cause the radar displays watched by Hawke firing unit personnel

23:54.020 --> 23:58.420
to appear exactly as they would in actual aircraft, either hostile or friendly, were

23:58.420 --> 23:59.780
in the area.

23:59.780 --> 24:04.660
The simulator is not nearly as costly as actual targets would be and provides training that

24:04.660 --> 24:11.420
would be difficult to give otherwise.

24:11.420 --> 24:17.700
Now signals simulating approaching hostile aircraft are beamed to battery control central.

24:17.700 --> 24:26.780
Inside, battery control personnel select a target and assign it to a specific launcher.

24:26.780 --> 24:42.620
The Hawke radars operate just as if actual aircraft were approaching.

24:42.620 --> 24:48.620
The Hawke swoops toward its imaginary prey.

24:48.620 --> 24:52.460
Battery control central personnel watch for the simulated intercepts.

24:52.460 --> 24:58.500
As usual, the faithful Hawke scores what would be a direct hit.

24:58.500 --> 25:03.700
Here personnel from a missile site defending a faraway city prepare for their annual practice

25:03.700 --> 25:30.900
firing of the Nike Hercules.

25:30.900 --> 25:42.420
As with the Hawke, a radar signal simulation station provides targets electronically.

25:42.420 --> 25:51.080
The search radars receive the simulated signals and react as they would to a real threat.

25:51.080 --> 25:55.660
In the director station, the radar displays show an enemy to be approaching.

25:55.660 --> 26:00.940
Based on the predicted kill point provided by the computer, a missile is selected for

26:00.940 --> 26:01.940
firing.

26:01.940 --> 26:17.980
As with the Hawke, the score is kept electronically and operators are effectively trained at minimum

26:17.980 --> 26:18.980
cost.

26:18.980 --> 26:26.460
Thus, the highlights of the Fort Bliss story, a story that ends with a traditional ceremony

26:26.460 --> 26:40.020
held in honor of the graduating air defense artillery man.

26:40.020 --> 26:46.780
These men are now ready to follow in the footsteps of the 200,000 graduates that have gone before.

26:46.780 --> 26:50.900
Whether they are called to serve in the frozen wastes of the Arctic, the barren stretches

26:50.900 --> 26:56.420
of the desert, or the devastating heat of the jungle, these men will maintain the perpetual

26:56.420 --> 26:59.620
vigilance demanded by our unsettled times.

26:59.620 --> 27:05.980
For they know that not only our national survival, but the survival of human freedom itself may

27:05.980 --> 27:20.860
largely depend upon the skillful use of our arsenal of air defense weapons.

27:20.860 --> 27:39.020
So you can see that these soldiers服 up for next feats.

